# .identity-denylist.example - the FORMAT of the operator denylist read by
# scripts/checks/check_identity.py. This example is tracked; a real denylist NEVER is.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. The gate's generic layer recognises personal data by its shape (private
# and tailnet IPs, *.ts.net names, C:\Users\<name> paths, email addresses). Your domain,
# your GitHub or git user name, your tailnet and machine names have no shape - they are
# words - so the gate can only recognise them if you tell it, locally.
#
# WHERE TO PUT YOURS (first found wins):
#   1. --denylist PATH, or the environment variable AI_STACK_IDENTITY_DENYLIST
#   2. .identity-denylist at the root of the checkout (gitignored)
#   3. identity-denylist inside the git directory - `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`,
#      usually .git/identity-denylist. RECOMMENDED: it can never be staged, and every
#      `git worktree` of the repository reads the same file.
# A newcomer needs none of this: with no denylist the gate runs its generic layer only,
# which is also what CI runs.
#
# FORMAT. One literal per line, matched case-insensitively ANYWHERE in a line (so a user
# name also catches it inside an email or a path). Blank lines and lines starting with #
# are ignored. Anything after ` #` is a label the gate prints INSTEAD of the value - a hit
# reads `operator (denylist entry 3 [tailnet])`, never the literal. Entries shorter than
# 4 characters are refused: they would match inside ordinary words.
#
# Example entries (synthetic - replace them with your own values):
example-domain.dev      # public domain
jdoe-example            # github / git user name
jdoe.example@mail.test  # personal email
tail0example            # tailnet name
jdoe-desktop-01         # machine name
